-- title:
--   Coefficientwise transport of the level field K₁· F₀
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime and $M'\ge 1$, and write $H\subseteq(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ for the kernel of reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/q)^\times$ (`levelH q M'`), $F=\mathbb Q$-function field `xHFunctionField (q^2*M') (levelH q M')` inside $\mathbb Q((q))$, and $\bar F=$ `fieldBar q M'` for the subfield of $\overline{\mathbb Q}((q))$ generated over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ by the coefficientwise image of $F$. Let $k_0$ be a subfield of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ containing $\mathbb Q$ and $K_1$ an intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/k_0$ with $K_1/k_0$ finite; $\bar F$ carries the $k_0$-algebra structure coming from $k_0\subseteq\overline{\mathbb Q}\subseteq\bar F$. Let $F_0$ be an intermediate field of $\bar F/k_0$ such that: (i) the compositum inside $\bar F$ of the $k_0$-subfield generated by all constants $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $F_0$ is all of $\bar F$; (ii) for every $m$, every $c:\mathrm{Fin}\,m\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$ linearly independent over $K_1$ and every $a:\mathrm{Fin}\,m\to\bar F$ with all $a_i$ in the compositum $K_1\cdot F_0$ (the $k_0$-subfield generated by the image of $K_1$, joined with $F_0$), the relation $\sum_i c_i a_i=0$ forces all $a_i=0$; (iii) every element of $\bar F$ whose Laurent series lies in the coefficientwise image of $\mathbb Q((q))$ belongs to $F_0$. Then there is a ring isomorphism $e$ from `laurentBaseChange K₁ (xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') (levelH q M'))`, the subfield of $K_1((q))$ generated over $K_1$ by the coefficientwise image of $F$, onto $K_1\cdot F_0$, such that for every $f$ the Laurent series over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ underlying $e(f)$ is the coefficientwise image of the series of $f$ under $K_1\hookrightarrow\overline{\mathbb Q}$.
--
--   This is the field-theoretic transport step identifying the level field of the $K_1$-model of $X_H(q^2M')$, realised inside $K_1((q))$ by $q$-expansions, with the subfield $K_1\cdot F_0$ of the geometric function field $\bar F\subseteq\overline{\mathbb Q}((q))$, the identification being the identity on $q$-expansions. The hypotheses (i)–(iii) are exactly the conditions — generation of $\bar F$ over the constants, linear disjointness from $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ over $K_1$, and containment of all rationally expanded elements — under which such a comparison holds, and the statement is used in the full-level development to move data between integral models and the geometric function field.
